## The shape

- Shortcut link, blank line, one or two sentences.
- Open with the change itself, usually as a participle: "Adding...",
  "Updating...", "Also gating...". Never "This PR...".
- The reason rides in a subordinate clause -- "so we can see whether folks are
  using the new syntax" -- rather than getting its own paragraph.
- `we` for the team and the product. Contractions throughout.
- A plain hyphen where a dash is wanted (`- then we use body2`), never an em
  dash.
- Casual register is correct. A small aside ("hoping this helps!") is in voice.
  Do not polish it into corporate prose, and do not fix my typos when quoting
  me.

## Length

One or two sentences is the default, because most changes are small and the diff
speaks for itself. Earn each paragraph past that. A non-obvious root cause is
worth a short one; an inventory of what changed is not, because the diff already
lists it.

## What earns its place

**Screenshots and video for anything visual.** A third of my PRs lead with an
image instead of an explanation, which is the right trade -- take it whenever
the change is something you can see.

**A before/after table**, when a change alters behaviour across several cases.
It earns its place by comparing, not by decorating:

| Range | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `now-6h` -> `now-5h` | `Loaded 5h ago` immediately | stale after 10m |
| `now-1h` -> `now+1h` | never shown | stale after 10m |

## What to leave out

Headings, bold, bulleted restatements of the diff, and any sentence describing
what kind of document this is.
